From tru64-unix-managers  Fri Oct 24 21:45:17 1997
From: "Jenkins, Gary  C." <gcjenkins () escocorp ! com>
Date: Fri, 24 Oct 1997 21:45:17 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY:  Oracle is being nice(d)
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078618110653

There are two areas to look at:

1) The kernel config and the /etc/sysconfigtab file.
	There is a kernel option called "autonice" (off by default) which will
cause any process that uses
	more than 600 seconds of CPU time (by default) to be "nice"d by the
autonice-penalty.  It is
	easiest to see the current values of these parameters by running
"dxkerneltuner" and looking in the
	"proc" section.  You can set the values to what you need them to be and
then reboot for those
	changes to take effect.  You may also turn on automatic nicing by
adding the line
		option	AUTONICE
	to the kernel configuration file under /sys/conf.  We had not changed
the default values and so
	this was not the problem for us.

2) The second area which does not depend upon the first is the
/var/adm/cron/queuedefs file
	This file sets parameters for jobs run by at, batch or cron and
background (sh, ksh, csh)
	jobs. This includes the nice value amoung other things.  This is what
the problem was for us.
	By making changes to the nice values, we were able to set things as we
wanted.

Thanks to:

