To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY  dupatch / RIS problems
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078618110596

Here's the original question:

	I've configured a RIS server running DU 4.0B using
	the new "clone" feature. Everything works fine except
	for the patch installation.  The remote patch directory
	mounts to the clients and setld installs the dupatch
	utility however the dupatch script expects the system to
	be in single user mode and it also expects the patches to
     	be on a local filesystem, not mounted via the network.
	Dupatch has an option to specify an alternative root path
	however this does not fix the problem

Here's the summary:

	Knut Hellebo's workaround,

As a quick workaround I've edited the lines requiring dupatch to be run
in single-user mode to install patches. I've installed 3.2c patches
(ALL) on several systems now from multi-user apparently problemfree
(meaning I have not seen any problems yet, even after kernel rebuild and
reboot). To be able to continue just set ERR=0 instead of ERR=1 in the
line below the "echo Requires single user ......"

	My workaround,

	Using Knuts' workaround, I then set up the postload file to copy
	the edited version of dupatch to the client after the patch
subset
	is installed.  Success!!

	Kris Chandrasekhar adds:

 It is my understanding that RIS support for dupatch will be available
in a future release. 
But on this issue I speak on my own, and you should contact 
Digital Customer Support Center 1-800-354-9000 to get the official
answer.


Thanks for the quick responses.
David Mongan
United Space Alliance

From tru64-unix-managers  Wed Oct 22 16:17:31 1997
From: "Robert L. McMillin" <rlm () syseca-us ! com>
Date: Wed, 22 Oct 1997 16:17:31 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: fdfs causes vdump to barf (4.0B)
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96078618110592

One of my users running 4.0B came to me with an odd question: his
nightly cron-scheduled vdump was failing.  I thought it strange, too --
we have the odd tape failure.  Usually, replacing the tape does the
trick, but not here.  What we found was that he had an fdfs filesystem
mounted in his tree, and it was confusing vdump!  That is to say, vdump
would exit if there was a fdfs filesystem mounted on a directory on the
same directory as root (say, at /fds).

I can't find anything in the mount(8) man page regarding fdfs, nor
anything that looks like it.  Why would vdump fail this way?  Normally,
it's smart enough to keep its paws off other filesystems.

